    {
      "id": "sol.hmmt-feb-algebra-2001-p2.hmmt",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Let \\(S=(x+1)\\left(x^{2}+1\\right)\\left(x^{4}+1\\right)\\left(x^{8}+1\\right) \\cdots=1+x+x^{2}+x^{3}+\\cdots\\). Since \\(x S=x+x^{2}+x^{3}+x^{4}+\\cdots\\), we have \\((1-x) S=1\\), so \\(S=\\frac{1}{1-x}\\).",
